Transactions - 
A transaction is a sequence of one or more database operations that are executed as a single unit of work.

A transaction must either:

Complete successfully (COMMIT)
Fail completely (ROLLBACK)

Transaction States - 
1. Active

Transaction is executing.

2. Partially Committed

All statements executed successfully.

Waiting for commit.

3. Committed

Changes permanently saved.

4. Failed

An error occurred.

Examples:

Constraint violation
System crash
Deadlock

5. Aborted

Database rolls back all changes.

COMMIT

Makes changes permanent.

ROLLBACK

Undoes all changes since transaction start.

A transaction is a sequence of database operations treated as a single logical unit of work. It ensures that either all operations are completed successfully and committed, or all changes are rolled back in case of failure. Transactions follow ACID properties to maintain data integrity and consistency.

