Group By VS having

	GROUP BY is used to group rows that have the same values in specified columns.

	HAVING is used to filter groups after grouping has been performed.

GROUP BY is used to group rows based on one or more columns, whereas HAVING is used to filter those groups after aggregation has been performed.

GROUP BY groups rows that have the same values into summary rows, while HAVING filters those groups after aggregation. WHERE filters individual rows before grouping, whereas HAVING filters aggregated results after GROUP BY. For example, HAVING COUNT(*) > 5 can filter groups based on aggregate values, which cannot be done using WHERE.

